---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProgramDashboard from '../../../components/charts/ProgramDashboard.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Applied Materials">
	<h1>Applied Materials</h1>
	<p class="dek">KPI dashboards and capital program coordination.</p>

	<section>
		<h3>Context</h3>
		<p>
			Applied Materials was running a multi-year capital program to support a new corporate
			office for over 1,200 employees, coordinating Finance, IT, R&amp;D, and outside contractor
			teams that didn't naturally share data or a single timeline view.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Problem</h3>
		<p>
			Schedule risk and budget status were scattered across teams, with no shared way for
			executives to see where the program actually stood without asking around.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Objective</h3>
		<p>
			Give leadership real-time visibility into schedule risk and budget utilization across the
			program, and keep budget planning and forecasting cycles aligned with Finance and
			Purchasing.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Constraints</h3>
		<p>
			Multi-million-dollar budget scrutiny, schedule risk spread across teams with different
			systems and reporting habits, and no existing shared dashboard to build from.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Your role</h3>
		<p>
			I built automated dashboards giving executives real-time visibility into schedule risk and
			budget utilization on the program, and led budget planning and forecast cycles with
			Finance and Purchasing, translating issues into decisions for senior leadership.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Approach</h3>
		<p>
			The dashboards pulled from the systems each team already used rather than asking them to
			adopt a new one, and surfaced risk and budget status without requiring a status meeting to
			get it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Artifacts</h3>
		<p>
			An illustrative program view reconstructed from the format actually used, not the real
			dashboard, all on synthetic sample numbers: a schedule-risk-by-workstream panel (Finance,
			IT, R&amp;D, contractor teams), an illustrative multi-workstream timeline showing how those
			four teams' schedules were coordinated across the program, and a budget-utilization-over-time
			trend tying to the forecast cycles mentioned above. Built as a small interactive panel, not a
			static image, click a workstream to see its status detail.
		</p>
		<ProgramDashboard />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Outcome</h3>
		<p>
			Leadership could see risk and budget status directly instead of asking around for it. No
			dollar or percentage figure is claimed here, since none was authorized for public use.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h3>Lesson</h3>
		<p>
			A dashboard is only as reliable as the data feeding it. Getting teams to keep their inputs
			current was its own ongoing effort, not a one-time build.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.dek {
		max-width: 42rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
	}

	section {
		margin-top: var(--space-4);
		max-width: 42rem;
	}

	section h3 {
		font-size: var(--text-base);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.02em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
	}
</style>
